We sit down with the entrepreneurs, investors and builders behind the most successful apps in the world to learn from their successes and failures.

3
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:22,480
Subclub is brought to you by RevenueCat.

4
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:31,920
Thousands of the world's best apps trust RevenueCat to power in app purchases, manage customers, and grow revenue across iOS and Android.

5
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:32,720
and the web.

6
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:38,240
You can learn more at revenuecat dot com dot Let's get into the show.

7
00:00:40,240 --> 00:00:45,120
Hello, I'm your host, David Barnard, and with me today, RevenueCat CEO, Jacob Biding.

8
00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:52,560
Our guest today is Paul Ganev, Vice President of Strategy, Business Development and Analytics at Surfline.

9
00:00:53,040 --> 00:01:06,480
On the podcast, we talk with Paul about the strategic pitfalls in modeling total addressable market, how freemium should work, and why Surfline's current success was actually 38 years in the making.

10
00:01:07,520 --> 00:01:10,160
Hey, Paul, thank you so much for joining us on the podcast today.

11
00:01:10,480 --> 00:01:11,760
I appreciate you guys having me on.

12
00:01:11,920 --> 00:01:12,880
I've been looking forward to it.

13
00:01:13,120 --> 00:01:15,040
And Jacob, always nice to chat with you.

14
00:01:15,760 --> 00:01:17,760
Just a guy in Ohio ready to talk about surfing.

15
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:19,120
That's what we're here for today.

16
00:01:19,520 --> 00:01:20,720
So let's talk surfing.

17
00:01:21,040 --> 00:01:22,560
I wanted to get a little background, Paul.

18
00:01:22,800 --> 00:01:29,040
So I know you're currently at Surfline, but you've done some really interesting things up to this that led to the job at Surfline.

19
00:01:29,280 --> 00:01:34,560
So tell us a little bit about what you did before we were in consumer subscriptions and then how you transitioned to Surfline?

20
00:01:34,880 --> 00:01:37,680
Yeah, I guess it's a non traditional entry into tech.

21
00:01:38,080 --> 00:01:46,960
So I worked in the media industry for the past eight years preceding my time in Surfline and I started off in the finance department working on traditional linear media.

22
00:01:47,440 --> 00:01:50,400
So broadcast media, cable, things like that.

23
00:01:50,560 --> 00:01:57,520
And two or three years after I started, Netflix really started taking up steam and we started seeing further adoption in spot viewership.

24
00:01:57,680 --> 00:02:01,040
And so I got really enamored with the streaming side of the business.

25
00:02:01,360 --> 00:02:13,280
And so a couple of years later, I got the opportunity to work on some of our SVOD products and I spent the last four or five years at Discovery Communications, which is now Warner Brothers Discovery working on some of their SVOD products.

26
00:02:13,840 --> 00:02:18,400
I transitioned from finance to strategy and analytics, got lucky enough to get a shop there.

27
00:02:18,720 --> 00:02:21,760
And then towards the end of my tenure, I had moved to LA.

28
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:27,200
I was originally based in London and I moved to LA with my wife and I started surfing a tremendous amount.

29
00:02:27,600 --> 00:02:30,960
I was surfing four or five times a week and I was using an app called Surfline.

30
00:02:31,280 --> 00:02:32,240
I got addicted to the product.

31
00:02:32,560 --> 00:02:37,760
I thought it was great and I got lucky enough to get a introduced to the CEO of Surfline at the time.

32
00:02:37,840 --> 00:02:40,080
His name was Jeff Berg and we had some conversations.

33
00:02:40,480 --> 00:02:46,880
They had just raised some capital from the churning group and he was looking to build out a strategy and analytics team at Surfline.

34
00:02:46,960 --> 00:02:54,080
I thought it was a great opportunity to work on something I was passionate about and get some experience in a different side of consumer subscription, right?

35
00:02:54,240 --> 00:02:58,480
Because SVOD was intrinsically a little different than mobile application subscription.

36
00:02:58,960 --> 00:03:00,320
I've been here for three years now.

37
00:03:00,640 --> 00:03:04,240
I oversee strategy analytics and then all of the other commercial functions.

38
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:08,160
So marketing, ad sales, product growth as well and loving it.

39
00:03:08,480 --> 00:03:10,240
What's the culture like at Surfline?

40
00:03:10,400 --> 00:03:13,200
Is it just a bunch of crazy surfers or kind of a mix?

41
00:03:13,920 --> 00:03:14,560
You would think so.

42
00:03:14,800 --> 00:03:16,080
And that was kind of my perception.

43
00:03:16,240 --> 00:03:20,160
I thought we'd come in and it would be that stereotypical surf culture that you'd see.

44
00:03:20,480 --> 00:03:27,920
And there's some of that, but I was surprised when I came in at how much talent there was at the company and how many tech native people there were.

45
00:03:28,560 --> 00:03:36,320
We had at the time our CMO came from Apple, our CO at the time, Kyle Laughlin had come from Amazon, and there were all these people had worked in big tech for many years.

46
00:03:36,480 --> 00:03:37,200
And so there was this.

47
00:03:37,680 --> 00:03:48,400
half culture of we're really focused on surf culture, surf community, but there was also a work hard mentality and people really wanted to innovate and build products that would make surfers' lives much better.

48
00:03:48,560 --> 00:03:58,000
And kind of in the surf industry, there aren't many jobs where you have a great balance between culture and innovation and that's what we found at Surfline and it's been fantastic.

49
00:03:58,480 --> 00:04:01,840
It's funny, coming into RevenueCat was kind of the opposite.

50
00:04:02,080 --> 00:04:11,040
Like everyone else at RevenueCat surfed and my first experience with RevenueCat in person was a little surf offsite in 2019.

51
00:04:12,240 --> 00:04:14,480
It was the last offsite we could do at a beach.

52
00:04:14,720 --> 00:04:17,760
We went from five out of five surfers to like five out of seven.

53
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:19,520
And I was like, this is over for us.

54
00:04:19,840 --> 00:04:20,960
It was just serendipitous.

55
00:04:21,120 --> 00:04:24,000
But the first five of us actually bonded over surfing.

56
00:04:24,320 --> 00:04:25,200
Also apps though.

57
00:04:25,360 --> 00:04:26,320
But I mean, it's interesting.

58
00:04:26,480 --> 00:04:40,640
It doesn't surprise me that especially I think California being the tech hub of the world more or less and also being one of the best surfing coasts in the world, if not the best, you end up with a lot of folks that are probably passionate about both of those things.

59
00:04:40,880 --> 00:04:51,600
And Surfline as a product, like there are other players in surf technology, but I don't think anyone is at the level that Surfline is in terms of reach and dominance and functionality and all of those things.

60
00:04:51,840 --> 00:04:56,880
So I'm sure, David, do you know that I mean, I'm going to steal your story, Paul, but the story of Surfline, you know how it got started?

61
00:04:57,280 --> 00:04:58,400
It was originally a phone call.

62
00:04:58,800 --> 00:05:00,560
Like, that's where Surfline comes from.

63
00:05:00,960 --> 00:05:01,280
That's right.

64
00:05:01,520 --> 00:05:04,960
It started as like an answering machine in someone's garage or something like this.

65
00:05:05,200 --> 00:05:06,960
Yeah, it was like a paper phone number that you would call.

66
00:05:07,280 --> 00:05:08,240
There was actually a fax as well.

67
00:05:08,320 --> 00:05:08,880
You could get a fax.

68
00:05:09,280 --> 00:05:10,080
Oh, that was great.

69
00:05:10,560 --> 00:05:11,680
It was like in the 80s, right?

70
00:05:11,760 --> 00:05:12,880
It's been like 85.

71
00:05:13,120 --> 00:05:15,120
Yeah, 1985 is when it was founded.

72
00:05:15,600 --> 00:05:22,640
Consumer Subscription App in 2023 founded in 1985 as a 1900 number.

73
00:05:23,120 --> 00:05:23,760
It might be a record.

74
00:05:24,240 --> 00:05:25,360
I think we hold a bunch of records.

75
00:05:25,520 --> 00:05:27,680
We call ourselves the 38 year old startup, right?

76
00:05:27,920 --> 00:05:32,480
So we're founded in 1985 and originally it was a paper phone number and then fax.

77
00:05:32,640 --> 00:05:38,560
And then when the web started to become a thing, when the internet started coming to its prominence, we had a free website for a period of time.

78
00:05:39,120 --> 00:05:41,520
And we actually launched our subscription in 2015.

79
00:05:42,480 --> 00:05:46,800
So to kind of date that, that's six years before Netflix launched a subscription.

80
00:05:47,360 --> 00:05:49,360
So we're better than Netflix in at least one way.

81
00:05:49,600 --> 00:05:56,480
At least the founders had the foresight to move in that direction pretty early, but there's been a serious evolution even since 2001.

82
00:05:56,560 --> 00:05:57,680
But yes, it's a crazy story.

83
00:05:57,760 --> 00:05:58,640
It's very unconventional.

84
00:05:58,880 --> 00:06:01,120
Yeah, I thought all trails was early to subscriptions.

85
00:06:01,360 --> 00:06:06,160
I didn't realize you all started your first consumer subscription product in 2001.

86
00:06:06,720 --> 00:06:07,280
That's crazy.

87
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:10,240
Yeah, it wasn't really a key focus back then in 2001.

88
00:06:10,480 --> 00:06:12,240
If you think about the market back then, there weren't many.

89
00:06:12,800 --> 00:06:14,560
digital subscription products that were out there.

90
00:06:14,720 --> 00:06:21,680
So it was a challenging proposition in the beginning to get people comfortable with a recurring payment through some digital product on the web.

91
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:23,120
And so it took many years to kind of.

92
00:06:23,600 --> 00:06:25,520
And technologically, like, how did you even do it?

93
00:06:25,920 --> 00:06:31,600
It's not like today when there's tools and all this stuff to make payments so much easier than it was twenty years ago.

94
00:06:31,920 --> 00:06:34,160
100% and again, it predeeds me quite a bit.

95
00:06:34,240 --> 00:06:37,040
So I can't say, but they definitely hacked some stuff together and got it working.

96
00:06:37,760 --> 00:06:45,760
Well, I think that's a good segue into the first topic I wanted to discuss, and that's understanding and calculating TAM, total addressable market.

97
00:06:46,640 --> 00:06:56,560
In 1985, I don't know that the founders could have ever imagined how the TAM would grow, but then also what it would actually look like in 2023.

98
00:06:57,040 --> 00:07:03,920
So, yes, tell me about your thinking on TAM and how to calculate it and common mistakes on how people think about TAM.

99
00:07:04,480 --> 00:07:06,960
TAM is really important for a company like Surfline.

100
00:07:07,280 --> 00:07:12,080
When you think about the total addressable market, how many people are there out there that might be willing to pay for your product?

101
00:07:12,400 --> 00:07:14,240
There are not a hundred million surfers in the US.

102
00:07:14,240 --> 00:07:14,560
1.5 million surfers in the US.

103
00:07:14,880 --> 00:07:15,680
So we have a niche TAM.

104
00:07:16,080 --> 00:07:23,200
It's very important we understand what that addressable market is because it should influence our product strategy, our monetization strategy, all those things.

105
00:07:23,520 --> 00:07:25,680
I'm sure the founders didn't think about TAM back then, right?

106
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:27,280
Yeah, they didn't have to pitch anyone.

107
00:07:27,520 --> 00:07:30,880
I mean, look, surf forecasting wasn't really a thing back in 1985.

108
00:07:31,040 --> 00:07:31,840
We kind of pioneered.

109
00:07:32,080 --> 00:07:38,800
Yeah, I learned that recently that even like wave forecasting wasn't until recent times that they even could predict when there were going to be swells and things like that.

110
00:07:38,960 --> 00:07:39,360
Yeah, blow.

111
00:07:39,520 --> 00:07:42,560
So big shout out to Sean Collins for doing that back in the eighties.

112
00:07:42,720 --> 00:07:44,000
But today it's really important for us.

113
00:07:44,400 --> 00:07:50,720
And when we think about TAM, really what we're trying to calculate is what is the size of the opportunity in the market.

114
00:07:51,120 --> 00:07:56,400
There are some great use cases for TAM, the big one being really like a sales tactic to be candid, right?

115
00:07:56,560 --> 00:08:01,200
From a TAM standpoint, you're going out to investors, talking about how big the opportunity is, telling a story there.

116
00:08:01,680 --> 00:08:11,520
The flaws come when you start trying to calculate the addressable audience from a modeling standpoint and what you should do strategically from product strategy and monetization strategy.

117
00:08:11,840 --> 00:08:12,640
So I'll give you an example.

118
00:08:12,880 --> 00:08:15,440
Let's say we're a golf company and you go out to market.

119
00:08:16,080 --> 00:08:18,480
And you say, okay, there's 50 million golfers in the US.

120
00:08:18,960 --> 00:08:19,680
That's our TAM.

121
00:08:19,920 --> 00:08:29,200
If you're launching a subscription product, you'll probably find pretty quickly after you launch the product that there's 50 million golfers out there, but there aren't 50 million golfers who play golf on a frequent basis.

122
00:08:29,360 --> 00:08:31,840
Maybe it's 20 million that actually play on a frequent basis.

123
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:35,200
And so you're kind of omitting a key factor, which is commitment.

124
00:08:35,840 --> 00:08:43,280
You really have to move from a metric TAM to another metric that's used in the industry called the serviceable addressable market SAM.

125
00:08:43,760 --> 00:08:57,520
And that's the biggest issue that I see with most companies that are going out to market and putting together their commercial strategy is they'll use Tam and they'll model everything upon that and they'll say, okay, well, Tam's 100 million people and we're going to go and acquire like five percent and call it a day.

126
00:08:57,680 --> 00:08:58,880
And that's how they model things out.

127
00:08:59,360 --> 00:09:07,440
But in reality, when you start digging in, you find out that, hey, really, our product isn't something that's going to serve all 100 million of these people.

128
00:09:07,680 --> 00:09:10,720
It's going to serve some fraction because there are other variables that I've omitted.

129
00:09:11,200 --> 00:09:15,040
And so it's really important to figure out what the serviceable addressable market is.

130
00:09:15,120 --> 00:09:17,200
And there's a number of different variables that you should look at.

131
00:09:17,360 --> 00:09:18,000
One is commitment.

132
00:09:18,400 --> 00:09:21,600
It's very important, especially in a niche direct to consumer space.

133
00:09:21,920 --> 00:09:28,080
You want people who are committed because they're going to be paying a subscription and that's often omitted in the modeling and the estimates that people put together.

134
00:09:28,720 --> 00:09:32,480
That's the biggest one to be candid that I see out when people are calculating the metric.

135
00:09:32,720 --> 00:09:33,920
How do you think about the SAM?

136
00:09:34,240 --> 00:09:37,120
Because I sometimes feel like SAM is actually the true Tam.

137
00:09:37,600 --> 00:09:39,760
You said like Tam is like the Tam people dream.

138
00:09:40,240 --> 00:09:41,680
Sam is actually your Tam.

139
00:09:42,160 --> 00:09:50,480
But I think as an argument, and maybe this goes into more long range modeling, if there's short range modeling, but that your SAM should maybe evolve into eating more of that Tam.

140
00:09:50,800 --> 00:09:52,800
Like it depends on what the limits of serviceability are, right?

141
00:09:52,880 --> 00:09:56,640
Like if it's commitment, golfers are not going to become twice as committed by 2028, right?

142
00:09:56,880 --> 00:10:03,920
Like I'm not going to read that in a Gartner report or whatever, but they might become twice as open to using software or something like that.

143
00:10:04,080 --> 00:10:09,440
So how do you think about Sam's evolution in regards to like serviceable versus addressable difference?

144
00:10:09,840 --> 00:10:11,600
I think it's all about timing, candidly.

145
00:10:11,840 --> 00:10:21,920
So you should always have Tam in the back of your mind because, you know, as a product leader in the space, you're going to look out and say, my product is going to continue to evolve serviceable for me in the future is different than what it is today.

146
00:10:22,320 --> 00:10:30,480
And so it's always important to have that TAM number in the back of your head and figure out what the delta is between your TAM and your currently serviceable addressable market.

147
00:10:31,120 --> 00:10:34,080
So I like using the serviceable addressable market for a couple of reasons.

148
00:10:34,160 --> 00:10:41,680
And the reason it's important more than anything is if you have a good idea of what's serviceable, it can really influence your commercial strategy.

149
00:10:41,920 --> 00:10:51,120
So another example is if your TAM is really small or your serviceable addressable market is really small, you might want to weigh your investment in ARPU more than you do subscription growth.

150
00:10:51,440 --> 00:10:55,520
You might say, okay, really, we might reach saturation a little bit faster than we originally thought.

151
00:10:55,760 --> 00:11:02,320
We should focus on growing ARPU or maybe the strategy is, hey, we know that we're going to get pretty significant market share within this audience.

152
00:11:02,480 --> 00:11:05,040
We should look at adjacent audiences or new geographies.

153
00:11:05,520 --> 00:11:08,320
And this is an exercise that a lot of big companies do.

154
00:11:08,560 --> 00:11:14,240
And if you look at the growth of companies like Spotify, for example, the growth isn't coming predominantly from the US anymore.

155
00:11:14,320 --> 00:11:17,680
It's they've realized, hey, we're starting to saturate a little bit in the US.

156
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:21,760
We're going to look at markets outside of the US and start getting market share there.

157
00:11:22,160 --> 00:11:24,080
The same thing with a lot of the big SPOD companies.

158
00:11:24,320 --> 00:11:31,760
So it's really important that you have your finger on the heartbeat there of what is actually serviceable and addressable today, but not lose sight of what is possible in the future.

159
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:40,960
Yeah, you could you use the word investment, but you end up putting effort into something that you can't change once you've hit the limits or at least like the reasonable limits of your channel or your market or whatever you're pulling into.

160
00:11:41,120 --> 00:11:42,000
It's just wasted money.

161
00:11:42,560 --> 00:11:56,880
Now on the acquisition side, revenue cat, I think we've had a similar experience where like I think if we had gone full into go to market three years ago, we probably would have had a lot of pain because of shortfalls in our product market fit and our ability to serve those customers and things like this.

162
00:11:57,120 --> 00:12:20,640
So maybe unintentionally, we strategically didn't, but now after three years of product development, okay, it's like now actually, maybe every dollar we invest in growth is probably going to have a much higher ROI now that the product's position that Sam has like increased for us, which kind of, I don't know, when you're thinking about managing a company over many years and benchmarking it against peers and benchmarking it against maybe peers outside of your space can be a little, I don't know, depressing.

163
00:12:20,720 --> 00:12:21,200
Sorry for the word.

164
00:12:21,280 --> 00:12:21,680
I don't know.

165
00:12:21,760 --> 00:12:29,200
Like sometimes you're like, I know for me personally, like I look at other stu SaaS companies that have essentially infinite TAMs, and they'll just be like, yeah, we're just doubling them every year.

166
00:12:29,280 --> 00:12:34,560
We spend 200 million dollars on sales and marketing, and then we add 200 million dollars in revenue and we just do it over and over again.

167
00:12:34,880 --> 00:12:38,560
And revenue cat exists in a niche in its own, which is this like consumer subscription.

168
00:12:38,720 --> 00:12:42,800
We have some of the same mechanics where it's not an infinite TAM, at least not today.

169
00:12:43,280 --> 00:12:47,280
I'm curious how you think about surfing too as the particular mechanics of it.

170
00:12:47,760 --> 00:12:54,960
There are some interesting natural limits to surfing's total addressable market because for anyone who's surfed, especially in some of the more crowded places in California.

171
00:12:55,440 --> 00:12:58,400
There's only a certain number of people that can surf practically at a spot.

172
00:12:58,560 --> 00:13:04,720
The laws of efficient markets really start to play out once a spot becomes too busy, can maybe only handle so much traffic.

173
00:13:04,960 --> 00:13:07,520
So there's a kind of a natural economic upper bound.

174
00:13:07,920 --> 00:13:11,360
Do you take that into your models, like how many surf spots are there that are undeveloped?

175
00:13:11,520 --> 00:13:16,240
And if you have that list of undeveloped surf spots, I would appreciate you sending it to me so I can go there.

176
00:13:16,560 --> 00:13:17,840
But we could save that for off the pod.

177
00:13:18,400 --> 00:13:21,120
We don't have a list of undeveloped surf spots, but you're right.

178
00:13:21,360 --> 00:13:24,960
It's a finite list of locations just like naturally in surfing.

179
00:13:25,200 --> 00:13:30,400
There's only a certain number of locations you can surf in and you're dependent on the conditions being reasonable for surfing.

180
00:13:30,480 --> 00:13:32,240
If it's flat, you can't actually get out.

181
00:13:32,640 --> 00:13:33,680
Also a few things though.

182
00:13:33,840 --> 00:13:35,840
Surfing is one of the sports that has been growing quite quickly.

183
00:13:36,160 --> 00:13:36,320
Right.

184
00:13:36,560 --> 00:13:40,080
It's had a crazy run since even the last ten years has been crazy.

185
00:13:40,400 --> 00:13:43,440
Not just surfing, but there's a lot of other sports that have seen the same adoption.

186
00:13:43,600 --> 00:13:46,800
I think golf and pickleball and all these companies, these sports have really been picking up.

187
00:13:46,960 --> 00:13:50,880
But in surfing now, we are seeing some other innovation that's interesting.

188
00:13:51,040 --> 00:13:52,880
There's building surf parks all around the world.

189
00:13:53,040 --> 00:13:56,560
So these are artificial wave pools where you can go surfing and it's interesting.

190
00:13:57,280 --> 00:14:00,800
because a lot of these pools are being built in places where people don't have access to the beach.

191
00:14:00,960 --> 00:14:08,800
So it could be right now, there's a huge pool being built in Munich, for example, and funny enough, there's a lot of German surfers who are just traveling to Morocco and Spain and different places.

192
00:14:09,120 --> 00:14:13,840
Every time I went to Central America, everyone at the surf camp was German, just to be sure.

193
00:14:13,920 --> 00:14:14,000
Yeah.

194
00:14:14,720 --> 00:14:18,400
We do see a lot of growth in the industry coming from those surf parks, right?

195
00:14:18,640 --> 00:14:19,920
It's going to drive a lot of adoption.

196
00:14:20,080 --> 00:14:33,360
And again, with demographics that are not traditional in surfing, which is also very interesting because these are urban areas, people don't have access to the beach, obviously living close to the be beach is very expensive, so this might bring in cohorts of surfers who couldn't traditionally afford to go surfing.

197
00:14:33,680 --> 00:14:34,560
It's a challenge, sure.

198
00:14:34,880 --> 00:14:42,160
Well, especially if you're a surf forecaster, because if you create a wave pool that has consistent waves, it must create some interesting product strategy challenges for you guys.

199
00:14:42,640 --> 00:14:43,360
It does to a degree.

200
00:14:43,520 --> 00:14:44,800
We kind of have two parts of the business.

201
00:14:45,120 --> 00:14:46,240
One is surf forecasting.

202
00:14:46,400 --> 00:14:49,760
The second piece are the cameras and the camera technology that we're developing.

203
00:14:49,840 --> 00:14:53,680
And there are some interesting synergies between the camera technology we're developing and surf parks.

204
00:14:53,760 --> 00:14:55,200
So I don't want to give away too much here.

205
00:14:55,600 --> 00:15:05,600
But this is where, twenty, thirty years in, you guys can leverage your a brand into that market shift, which not all companies of all scales have that luxury, but there's a good example of widening your serviceable addressable market, right?

206
00:15:05,760 --> 00:15:17,360
Like looking at the Tam from a top down perspective, what's going to shift and surf ranches and surf parks are, I think, going to be I live in Ohio, I would really love for someone to put an indoor surf park up within five hours.

207
00:15:17,520 --> 00:15:20,160
That would change everything for me to continue my hobby.

208
00:15:20,480 --> 00:15:24,560
You know, in terms of strategy, that's the kind of stuff you want to be thinking about with Tam, I feel.

209
00:15:25,040 --> 00:15:28,800
I'm curious, when I learned computing in addressable market, I was taught two methods, right?

210
00:15:29,360 --> 00:15:30,320
There's the top down method.

211
00:15:30,400 --> 00:15:42,960
Well, like there's a million surfers and like we'll get whatever versus like a bottoms up, which maybe I have these wrong, but it's like, well, I only need to get five of these people to like whatever and I can sell this many this year and whatever and I'll build up to how many I can reasonably sell.

212
00:15:43,600 --> 00:15:50,080
Do you find when you're approaching a problem, do you start with six billion people and what percentage of those are surfers or do you start from the bottom up?

213
00:15:50,480 --> 00:15:54,720
For most companies, I think they start top down traditionally, mainly because the data is easier to come by.

214
00:15:55,040 --> 00:15:56,720
It's a easier exercise to do, right?

215
00:15:56,960 --> 00:16:00,480
So you can hire a company to go out and do market research for you and get you some estimates.

216
00:16:00,720 --> 00:16:03,920
But candidly, there's so much market research out there today that I'm surprised.

217
00:16:04,160 --> 00:16:07,440
Most industries you should be able to find some data points to get you started.

218
00:16:07,600 --> 00:16:11,360
Yes, because if you can't find those numbers, you're probably in a market you don't understand enough.

219
00:16:12,080 --> 00:16:13,440
Yeah, or it's too broad, right?

220
00:16:13,680 --> 00:16:14,720
Yeah, you're absolutely right.

221
00:16:14,880 --> 00:16:22,880
But the exercise is really this, at least the way that I've found it to be most successful is you find some market research out there and it gives you a general sense of what the scale of the market is.

222
00:16:23,440 --> 00:16:30,880
And then if you have great analytics on your platform, you can learn enough about your consumers to figure out what segmentation actually matters.

223
00:16:31,520 --> 00:16:40,320
Like what your SAM should be, because the market research that you find out there is typically just going to be very high level and you're going to have to cut it down, but it's up to you to determine how to cut it down.

224
00:16:40,720 --> 00:16:42,240
What variables do you need to factor in?

225
00:16:42,400 --> 00:16:45,680
Are there geographical variables, demographic variables, is it commitment?

226
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:56,080
So it's important that you kind of go from both angles, start with market research, do some analysis of your consumer, and then try to hone in on what a serviceable addressable market is.

227
00:16:56,240 --> 00:16:58,320
And you can make it as complex or high level as you want.

228
00:16:58,400 --> 00:17:03,040
I mean, the moment you start factoring in competitors, it starts getting really complicated because you have to factor in in.

229
00:17:03,520 --> 00:17:10,880
That's kind of the next step down, which is serviceable, obtainable market is what percentage of the market can you actually acquire compared to the other competitors in the market.

230
00:17:11,120 --> 00:17:13,040
I think it's probably a little bit detailed for this conversation.

231
00:17:13,520 --> 00:17:16,320
But we talk about modeling a lot and modeling's wrong.

232
00:17:16,560 --> 00:17:16,720
Always.

233
00:17:17,360 --> 00:17:23,680
I find that on the line of complexity, I'm always saying the more complex you make a model, the more likely you're going to get it to say what you want it to say.

234
00:17:23,840 --> 00:17:25,280
And the more likely it is to be wrong.

235
00:17:25,760 --> 00:17:25,920
Right?

236
00:17:26,160 --> 00:17:26,800
Simple is better.

237
00:17:27,040 --> 00:17:30,080
If you can start with the simplest model and get answers that are reasonable.

238
00:17:30,320 --> 00:17:35,840
And then I don't know if you found this, but I find that it's useful to not just revisit the same model every time you need it.

239
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:41,200
But if your model is so incomplex that you can't kind of rebuild it in a sitting, then your model might be too complex.

240
00:17:41,440 --> 00:17:47,920
Because what I also find nice is that if you start from first principles and rebuild your models once in a while, one, you'll rethink your assumptions.

241
00:17:48,320 --> 00:17:56,000
Two, if you get an answer that reasonably matches your first attempt, then you have some confidence that you know, you didn't just torture your model to say what you wanted it to say.

242
00:17:56,400 --> 00:18:00,800
I mean, that applies not just for addressable market modeling, that's for building any kind of predictive thing.

243
00:18:01,120 --> 00:18:07,840
But yeah, it's definitely something for our listeners in Appland, we have probably a lot of people that aren't thinking about having to pitch investors, right?

244
00:18:08,080 --> 00:18:10,960
Having to tell some massive TAM story, but it doesn't matter.

245
00:18:11,120 --> 00:18:21,920
You're the investor, you're investing your time, do some math and make sure that you have a TAM that seems pretty big to you, because if the smaller your TAM, less that TAM is going to grow, the harder time you're going to have.

246
00:18:22,080 --> 00:18:22,960
It's just going to be tougher.

247
00:18:23,120 --> 00:18:26,800
And if you have a small enough TAM, even winning isn't that good of a result, right?

248
00:18:27,120 --> 00:18:28,800
The TAM is small enough, you know what I mean?

249
00:18:29,120 --> 00:18:35,120
So I think it's especially for hobbyists looking to like build something, do yourself a favor, build something a lot of people can use.

250
00:18:37,040 --> 00:18:41,680
Or build something a small niche wants to use, but then understand that it is a small niche.

251
00:18:41,760 --> 00:18:41,840
Yeah.

252
00:18:42,160 --> 00:18:45,040
And then charge appropriately to the size of that market.

253
00:18:45,520 --> 00:18:47,920
But I did want to dig into service obtainable market.

254
00:18:48,160 --> 00:19:00,240
I know you kind of said it's a little too complex to dive into, but I think this is an important thing that is part of the modeling that is often overlooked is that, okay, there's fifty million surfers or that's probably even high.

255
00:19:00,640 --> 00:19:16,080
That's the Tam or Sam is actually like twenty million, but then you have like willingness to pay and pricing and all those other things that factor in like how many of those folks in the SAM are you actually going to convert to a subscriber and that's the obtainable.

256
00:19:16,480 --> 00:19:18,400
So how do you think about that willingness to pay?

257
00:19:18,560 --> 00:19:23,360
And then I'm going to out Jacob and some of the early RevenueCat employees, surfers are notoriously cheap.

258
00:19:23,600 --> 00:19:27,280
I think the first five employees at RevenueCat were like sharing a Surfline account.

259
00:19:27,440 --> 00:19:31,920
So you have my Surfline account, by the way, just want to point out that I was the payer.

260
00:19:32,080 --> 00:19:33,520
I was always paying and I have one now.

261
00:19:33,600 --> 00:19:39,120
Even though I live in Ohio, I have almost always an annual Surfline subscription going because it will expire.

262
00:19:39,200 --> 00:19:41,360
And then the next trip I take to the coast, I re-subscribe.

263
00:19:41,440 --> 00:19:42,560
So I've made up for my sins.

264
00:19:42,880 --> 00:19:44,800
So how do you factor in that willingness to pay?

265
00:19:44,960 --> 00:19:54,000
And then obviously the goal for Surfline is to continue to evolve the product and to get more and more of that service addressable market to be service obtainable market.

266
00:19:54,400 --> 00:20:00,240
So yeah, how do you think about that willingness to pay in modeling from Tam to Sam to some?

267
00:20:00,800 --> 00:20:00,880
Yeah.

268
00:20:02,080 --> 00:20:04,160
So maybe we need to couple them a little bit.

269
00:20:04,480 --> 00:20:06,720
So really what we're talking about is price sensitivity, right?

270
00:20:06,960 --> 00:20:08,320
How price sensitive a consumer is.

271
00:20:08,640 --> 00:20:13,840
And even that term is maybe a little bit misleading because it has three components, at least the way I like to think about it.

272
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:16,400
First is purchasing power, which is what we're traditionally talking about.

273
00:20:16,560 --> 00:20:21,440
So if you say like surfers are cheap, really what you mean is that it may be a purchasing power situation possibly.

274
00:20:21,840 --> 00:20:25,840
The other two variables are commitment, like how committed are they, how passionate are they?

275
00:20:25,920 --> 00:20:28,480
And the last is how strong is your value proposition?

276
00:20:28,880 --> 00:20:34,960
It's really a relationship between those three things, and you have to assess how strong that relationship is and where the gaps are.

277
00:20:35,120 --> 00:20:43,600
So in surfing, for example, if you think about where most surfers, like let's say domesticallyally, where they're located, typically in California coastal areas or Hawaiian coastal areas.

278
00:20:43,680 --> 00:20:45,680
And so those areas are not cheap to live in.

279
00:20:45,920 --> 00:20:51,360
And when we've done market research, we found that actually the audience is a bit more affluent than you would originally believe.

280
00:20:51,760 --> 00:20:56,400
Now when you go to commitment, like I have not met a more committed, passionate group of people than surfers.

281
00:20:56,720 --> 00:21:02,800
These are people who wake up at four in the morning to drive two hours away to catch a swell window of an hour and go surfing.

282
00:21:02,960 --> 00:21:04,080
And then go to work after that.

283
00:21:04,320 --> 00:21:04,400
Yeah.

284
00:21:04,560 --> 00:21:09,120
And then go to work right after like sitting in a five mm wetsuit in 55 degree water for a couple of hours.

285
00:21:09,200 --> 00:21:10,720
So commitment level super high.

286
00:21:11,120 --> 00:21:13,840
And then the last piece is like how strong your value proposition is.

287
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:15,840
That's the kind of relationship we're talking about.

288
00:21:16,240 --> 00:21:26,480
So if you think about Sam and Psong in that kind of light, really the first thing to think about is like how strong your value proposition really is and then over time, how much stronger do you believe it's going to get?

289
00:21:26,880 --> 00:21:36,560
That's a huge benefit of freemium models because over time as your value proposition gets stronger, you have the ability or the time benefit of being able to convert people that are on the platform.

290
00:21:37,520 --> 00:21:45,840
And so that's the first big one is like how strong is your value proposition and how much stronger is your it going to get over time and how does that compare to the other value propositions in the market?

291
00:21:46,160 --> 00:21:50,640
So for Surfline, for example, there are a lot of free competitors out there, there's not many paid competitors.

292
00:21:51,280 --> 00:21:56,240
And so we're targeting a consumer that would be willing to pay for this product.

293
00:21:56,480 --> 00:22:02,240
So the commitment level has to be pretty high compared to someone who surfs maybe once a month or once every couple of months.

294
00:22:02,560 --> 00:22:05,760
Maybe the free products that are there on the market are a better fit for them.

295
00:22:05,920 --> 00:22:13,040
However, over time, we have planned to produce a product that would be a better fit for these other consumers that are targeted by other audiences.

296
00:22:13,200 --> 00:22:20,880
So there's no one size fits all, but you really need to make sure that you're aligned with those three variables and understand really where the gaps are and what you're trying to influence.

297
00:22:21,360 --> 00:22:38,320
When strategically thinking about some, how much do you just write off like a certain percentage of surfers, no matter what the value prop, just won't be willing to pay, or is it a price sensitivity where it's less that they're not willing to pay anything, it's just that they're not willing to pay the price that makes sense for a surf line?

298
00:22:39,120 --> 00:22:42,960
I'm pretty certain if all the Surfline cams were free, everyone would just use it.

299
00:22:43,920 --> 00:22:45,760
Because there's not a ton of other cams.

300
00:22:46,720 --> 00:22:46,800
Yeah.

301
00:22:47,040 --> 00:22:48,480
Again, it's a hard thing to calculate.

302
00:22:48,640 --> 00:23:02,560
I think the way that we go about it is we have a pretty large free audience and we can do quite a bit of analysis and understand how much purchasing power, price sensitivity affects people's conversion rates and how that changes over time as our product gets better.

303
00:23:03,120 --> 00:23:06,160
There will always be a cohort of users that's not willing to pay.

304
00:23:06,560 --> 00:23:09,600
And it's especially important for a company like Surfline, right?

305
00:23:09,760 --> 00:23:11,520
Because Surfline historically was free.

306
00:23:11,760 --> 00:23:16,080
There's a lot of companies that have gone through this transition where they had a free product and now it's behind a paywall.

307
00:23:16,240 --> 00:23:33,120
So New York Times is another good example and that intrinsically causes challenges because you've educated a consumer that something used to be free and now it's something that they have to pay for, which again also is something that influences whether somebody is willing to pay for something or not because again they just believe it should be free.

308
00:23:33,520 --> 00:23:37,760
So at least in my mind, there's no rule of thumb, but there will always be cohorts of users.

309
00:23:37,920 --> 00:23:43,040
It doesn't matter what industry it is and it doesn't matter how much purchasing power they have that will not be willing to pay for the product.

310
00:23:43,520 --> 00:23:49,440
And so you just do your best to quantify how large that cohort is, either through surveying or through unproductive analysis.

311
00:23:50,080 --> 00:23:53,360
And then you factor that into your models, which will intrinsically be wrong.

312
00:23:53,360 --> 00:23:55,120
So then we'll make a decision and go from there.

313
00:23:55,520 --> 00:24:00,160
This did get really nerdy, but these are the things as a consumer subscription business you need to be thinking about.

314
00:24:00,400 --> 00:24:01,120
And I think you're right.

315
00:24:01,280 --> 00:24:03,680
You kicked it off saying you see a lot of mistakes.

316
00:24:03,840 --> 00:24:04,800
Like I see that too.

317
00:24:04,960 --> 00:24:08,560
A lot of people like, oh yeah, there's fifty million golfers in the US.

318
00:24:08,960 --> 00:24:11,760
We only need five percent penetration or whatever to build a great business.

319
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:13,200
And then we're going to get to fifty percent.

320
00:24:13,280 --> 00:24:17,360
And me in twenty eighteen being like, oh, there's a 100 billion dollars in subscription revenue.

321
00:24:17,520 --> 00:24:18,640
I just need to get one percent of that.

322
00:24:18,720 --> 00:24:24,560
And then you learn, well, 85 percent of that is locked up in four apps and they are never touching their inventory again.

323
00:24:24,800 --> 00:24:27,120
So, like, okay, now my numbers change a little.

324
00:24:27,440 --> 00:24:32,880
Maybe the benefit of naivete in those early days, because if you totally understood your challenges, you might never start.

325
00:24:33,200 --> 00:24:34,960
No one ever got hurt from knowing more, right?

326
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:46,720
By going through the modeling exercise as we've discussed, and I think you've had some really great thoughts that I hope our audience can take away from this to do their own modeling to just understand where they are.

327
00:24:46,720 --> 00:24:51,120
Understand where their business is going and understand where to invest and how much to charge.

328
00:24:51,520 --> 00:25:10,000
And taking those three variables you shared, it's like if you understand that your service addressable market is much smaller, then, like you said, you do need to focus more on ARPU and build something that's super valuable to that smaller service addressable market versus trying to be the twenty dollars a year, everybody's going to subscribe.

329
00:25:10,080 --> 00:25:13,360
Well, everybody's not going to subscribe even if it is twenty dollars a year.

330
00:25:13,760 --> 00:25:17,920
And if your market is small, you don't want to be twenty dollars a year, you want to be one hundred twenty dollars a year.

331
00:25:18,720 --> 00:25:22,800
So I think this is all super important for anyone in this industry to be thinking about.

332
00:25:23,040 --> 00:25:26,240
And then that leads into the next topic I wanted to cover is freemium strategy.

333
00:25:26,240 --> 00:25:29,520
So you understand, okay, a certain percentage are not going to pay.

334
00:25:30,080 --> 00:25:31,920
They're part of our service addressable market.

335
00:25:32,160 --> 00:25:35,040
They're going to get some value from our product even if they're not willing to pay.

336
00:25:35,440 --> 00:25:48,960
So how do you approach that at Surfline of offering some amount of value for free to those users who don't want to pay or do have a level of price sensitivity that you're not delivering quite enough value to get them to pay?

337
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:57,040
When you think about freemium, really what the hypothesis is, is that you can launch this freemium model that over time will reduce your CAC.

338
00:25:57,200 --> 00:26:02,640
That's the goal is, hey, I'm building this model that will result in lower customer acquisition cost than if I didn't have it.

339
00:26:03,040 --> 00:26:06,960
And there's a lot of things to factor in when determining whether that's true or not, right?

340
00:26:07,120 --> 00:26:10,640
Because both models, a freemium model or a completely paid model, can be successful.

341
00:26:10,720 --> 00:26:12,880
And there's thousands of examples for Surfline.

342
00:26:13,200 --> 00:26:18,560
It's kind of funny again because we didn't originally get into the business and say, hey, we're going to have this freemium model, right?

343
00:26:18,640 --> 00:26:21,680
We were free and then we transitioned to paid over a long period of time.

344
00:26:22,560 --> 00:26:30,960
But because we started as a free product over the years, we accumulated a very large audience on the platform that was habituated to the products that we were offering.

345
00:26:31,680 --> 00:26:40,160
And it was really important for an industry like surf, because if you're a new surfer getting into the sport, you don't really know why surf forecasting matters, right?

346
00:26:40,400 --> 00:26:41,520
Like you're going to go when you go.

347
00:26:41,680 --> 00:26:42,960
You don't understand why wind is so bad.

348
00:26:43,360 --> 00:26:48,480
I learned everything I know about predicting surf from the human written surf forecasts for the Bay Area from Surfline.

349
00:26:48,560 --> 00:26:49,440
But you talk about habitual.

350
00:26:49,520 --> 00:26:54,320
First thing in the morning surfing or not, pull up my phone before I look at Twitter, I'd open surfline.

351
00:26:54,720 --> 00:26:55,280
Yeah, yeah.

352
00:26:55,600 --> 00:27:01,840
It was a great strategy because over time what we did was we habituated users and we educated them on the products that we offered.

353
00:27:02,640 --> 00:27:14,960
And so we ended up creating this great funnel that allowed us to educate users on the free product and then over time as they found value in what we were offering, they would want the full suite of product features and then subscribe.

354
00:27:15,120 --> 00:27:24,480
And so we've ended in a place today where we don't spend any money on paid acquisition and we have this great growth funnel that effectively where we're're organically getting people into the free user experience.

355
00:27:24,960 --> 00:27:32,080
We educate them, habituate them, and then there's some cohort of surfers that get very committed and love the product and end up subscribing.

356
00:27:32,320 --> 00:27:34,080
And so we built this great growth engine.

357
00:27:34,400 --> 00:27:35,200
I will say a few things.

358
00:27:35,360 --> 00:27:37,120
One is like this doesn't happen overnight.

359
00:27:37,200 --> 00:27:44,560
So if you're contemplating starting a free or a freemium model business, there's going to be pain in the beginning for sure, for a few reasons.

360
00:27:44,640 --> 00:27:46,880
The first being that you're adding an extra step, right?

361
00:27:47,040 --> 00:27:53,440
If people just hit a paywall, there's some percentage that's just going to convert and move through, but you're adding an extra step, a free experience.

362
00:27:53,520 --> 00:27:55,920
It's going to take time for people to understand what you're offering.

363
00:27:56,320 --> 00:27:58,640
You're going to get it wrong a bunch of times at the beginning.

364
00:27:58,960 --> 00:28:00,400
You have to draw a bunch of borders too.

365
00:28:00,640 --> 00:28:01,280
I always found it difficult.

366
00:28:01,520 --> 00:28:06,160
Like every part of your product has to have a line in it somewhere and it gets really complicated to manage.

367
00:28:06,560 --> 00:28:07,120
It absolutely does.

368
00:28:07,280 --> 00:28:09,920
And again, you're going to have to test and iterate and test and iterate.

369
00:28:10,160 --> 00:28:16,720
And there's a kind of magic rule here with freemium models that I tell everyone to try and follow.

370
00:28:17,360 --> 00:28:22,400
You're really trying to balance increasing conversion versus increasing free user retention.

371
00:28:22,640 --> 00:28:23,680
It's very important that you do that.

372
00:28:24,640 --> 00:28:28,960
If you don't do that, your premium model will most likely not be a success.

373
00:28:29,520 --> 00:28:30,720
And here's why I say that, right?

374
00:28:30,960 --> 00:28:37,920
So you launch a premium model and over time you see that, hey, we've optimized our premium model and now conversions doubled, fantastic.

375
00:28:38,640 --> 00:28:44,320
But it's coming at the cost of free user retention and we're not retaining free users nearly as effectively as we were before.

376
00:28:44,640 --> 00:28:53,520
And so what will happen is every month, if the retention is poor enough, the size of your free audience begins to decline and the pool of free users that you can convert to a paid product gets smaller.

377
00:28:53,920 --> 00:28:57,760
So it doesn't matter if you double the conversion rate on half the audience, you haven't gone anywhere.

378
00:28:58,160 --> 00:28:58,400
Exactly.

379
00:28:58,560 --> 00:29:05,360
And that's the biggest issue that I see with freemium models out in the industry is there hasn't been enough testing and they've waited too high on one versus the other.

380
00:29:05,680 --> 00:29:13,440
Now, if you have to wait more on one than the other, it's probably better to wait on free user retention if you have the capital to sustain less conversion for a period of time.

381
00:29:13,680 --> 00:29:18,640
Because as a product owner or whoever, you have to believe that your product is going to improve over time.

382
00:29:18,800 --> 00:29:24,560
And the great thing about a free experience is it buys you a bit of time to optimize your product and make it better.

383
00:29:25,200 --> 00:29:29,920
Because the ultimate role here is, okay, we're just trying to build great products that people want to pay for.

384
00:29:30,400 --> 00:29:34,480
And you'd much rather have people on your product rather than off market, right?

385
00:29:34,640 --> 00:29:46,880
If they come in and they hit a paywall and they don't want to subscribe, it's much harder to go out in the market and find them with social media and then convince them to come back versus on platform, your messaging is very clear, your value propositions are easy to communicate.

386
00:29:47,040 --> 00:29:51,200
And so there's a huge benefit in being able to communicate people as the product gets better, i.e.

387
00:29:51,360 --> 00:29:54,880
like Dualingo has absolutely smashed it in this category over the years.

388
00:29:54,960 --> 00:29:56,720
They've optimized and optimized, optimizedize.

389
00:29:57,120 --> 00:29:59,840
It still doesn't seem like a paid product even though it makes a ton of money, right?

390
00:30:00,240 --> 00:30:01,120
Such a magical hack.

391
00:30:01,280 --> 00:30:06,720
But they spent how long they were like six or seven years in market before they started to monetize in a real way is crazy.

392
00:30:07,120 --> 00:30:07,200
Yeah.

393
00:30:07,600 --> 00:30:10,400
And I can't tell you how much they've optimized that free experience.

394
00:30:10,560 --> 00:30:13,280
They've made it so fun and they habituate their users, right?

395
00:30:13,440 --> 00:30:18,080
So like learning isn't intrinsically something that all consumers come in and are like, this is fun, right?

396
00:30:18,400 --> 00:30:27,600
But Duolingo's found the way to optimize their experience in a way that you come in, you use the free product over time, you start having fun using the product and they've realized like that's the thing.

397
00:30:27,600 --> 00:30:33,200
They've realized like that's the best way to convert someone to a paying subscription, but it takes years and years and optimizations.

398
00:30:33,360 --> 00:30:35,200
And there's so many different freemium models, by the way.

399
00:30:35,280 --> 00:30:36,480
It's not just one model out there.

400
00:30:36,720 --> 00:30:38,080
Duolingo is a great example of that, right?

401
00:30:38,240 --> 00:30:41,600
They've had gems and a premium subscription and like, lots of different things that they've tried.

402
00:30:41,840 --> 00:30:42,240
I don't know.

403
00:30:42,320 --> 00:30:44,480
I'm sure they've experimented with ads at some point too.

404
00:30:44,640 --> 00:30:53,120
I don't know if it's in the full product, but we had a guest from Duolingo on a few months ago and the experimentation there is crazy, which is what you probably need, right?

405
00:30:53,280 --> 00:30:56,720
To like swim through that freemium universe and find an optimal.

406
00:30:57,040 --> 00:31:05,680
The behavior you mentioned of trading off between free user retention and conversion and then focusing on the product, makes me think of the sort of submodel.

407
00:31:05,840 --> 00:31:07,680
I always like to think about it's like value creation, right?

408
00:31:07,840 --> 00:31:09,200
Your app is going to create value.

409
00:31:09,760 --> 00:31:13,520
Conversion rates and paywalls and all this stuff is a motion of capturing value.

410
00:31:13,760 --> 00:31:17,600
And if you capture all the value, users won't hang around.

411
00:31:17,760 --> 00:31:20,240
There needs to be a value surplus that they're able to maintain.

412
00:31:20,640 --> 00:31:37,440
And I think that's what you're kind of describing there is if you squeeze those free users so that there's no value, there's not enough value there for them because you've shut it off or like closed it, you're actually going to end end up in a worse position versus spending your time actually just increasing the big pie that you're trying to capture value from, then your job becomes a little bit easier, right?

413
00:31:37,680 --> 00:31:44,880
I'm a fan of monetizing early because I do think it gives real numbers and real measurements on things, but that can go too far and not get enough people in.

414
00:31:45,360 --> 00:31:57,120
Also in the early, early days when you have just like small numbers, if your app is so paywalled that you can't get enough people in to really learn, you're going to have a hard time going anywhere because like you can't improve a product without users.

415
00:31:57,280 --> 00:31:59,600
Like I think a lot of folks like to pretend they're smart.

416
00:32:00,000 --> 00:32:06,000
And like Steve Jobs or whatever, and they can just tell you what's a great product, but I don't think even Steve did that really, right?

417
00:32:06,320 --> 00:32:08,800
Like everyone's doing it in conversation with users.

418
00:32:09,440 --> 00:32:14,560
It's hard with freemium because so many of the benefits of freemium are non P and L line items.

