00:32:54,720 --> 00:33:03,440
So when we write a surf report for a certain location, if surfers are going to a new area and trying to figure out where to surf, they'll google what are the surf conditions at at El Porto or wherever?

435
00:33:03,520 --> 00:33:06,240
And they'll land on Surfline or a competitor's page.

436
00:33:06,480 --> 00:33:11,760
So there's a lot of traffic coming into those spot pages and individual surf reports, but we do a lot of content as well.

437
00:33:11,920 --> 00:33:18,720
We have a big editorial arm that writes not just about surf conditions and kind of what's happening there, but we write a lot about news and other topics as well.

438
00:33:18,880 --> 00:33:18,960
Yeah.

439
00:33:19,120 --> 00:33:27,920
It's interesting what we tend to call free or organic when it's like we actually like RevenueCat doesn't do a lot of paid acquisition either, but we do a lot of work to get acquisition, right?

440
00:33:29,680 --> 00:33:40,880
How does the conversion over time play out for Surfline specifically because I've talked to a lot of app businesses where, oh yeah, if someone doesn't convert in that first session, they just never convert.

441
00:33:41,280 --> 00:33:51,680
But I've always kind of thought that means freemium probably isn't working, that's not a good freemium strategy if that is the case and it needs a rethink.

442
00:33:51,840 --> 00:34:00,000
So I would imagine that you do see some positive year over year growth in being able to convert those free users into paid subscribers.

443
00:34:00,320 --> 00:34:00,400
Yeah.

444
00:34:00,640 --> 00:34:04,000
So I think probably what that person is referring to and I see this with most products is.

445
00:34:04,560 --> 00:34:10,560
when people download the app, even if you have a freemium model, typically the largest percentage of your conversion will happen pretty quickly.

446
00:34:10,720 --> 00:34:14,400
Like people will convert within the first seven days, and then there's a trickle of conversion over time.

447
00:34:14,800 --> 00:34:23,680
Now, again, with freemium, like the hypothesis here is that the value that you put behind the paywall will continue to grow and the value of the product will continue to improve.

448
00:34:24,240 --> 00:34:30,240
And so it's up to you to articulate that value and make sure there's a balance between the value that's in front of the paywall and behind the paywall.

449
00:34:30,800 --> 00:34:36,560
So a lot of companies, that's where they fail is there's just a misbalance between the value in front and behind the paywall.

450
00:34:37,040 --> 00:34:38,400
And you need to do a lot of testing.

451
00:34:38,640 --> 00:34:42,400
The last three years we've tested and tested and tested different options, right?

452
00:34:42,720 --> 00:34:46,880
Not just messaging or what's paywalled and what's not, but the different models.

453
00:34:47,200 --> 00:34:52,960
As an example, there's a pretty typical model where a consumer gets unlimited access to a limited amount of features, right?

454
00:34:53,120 --> 00:34:56,480
They come in and like these three features are free, but you can use them as often as you want.

455
00:34:56,880 --> 00:35:04,480
There's another model that's pretty typical, the New York Times model where you come in and you get limited access to not a limited amount of features, but to some features.

456
00:35:04,560 --> 00:35:08,880
So you come in and after your third time using these features you're no longer allowed to use them.

457
00:35:09,120 --> 00:35:11,440
And you see this in gaming and all kinds of different industries.

458
00:35:11,680 --> 00:35:13,040
And there's pros and cons to both.

459
00:35:13,200 --> 00:35:14,000
And we've tested both.

460
00:35:14,480 --> 00:35:21,600
Ultimately, I'm a bigger fan of not limiting engagement because if you can create some kind of habituation with a free product, it's fantastic.

461
00:35:21,760 --> 00:35:22,640
They'll come in on their own.

462
00:35:22,720 --> 00:35:29,600
And if someone's coming in ten, fifteen times a month, that gives you more opportunity to message them, educate them about the product that you're offering.

463
00:35:30,240 --> 00:35:37,040
So there's some imbalance likely for a lot of these other companies, and you have to determine whether it's a value problem or if it's a model problem.

464
00:35:37,520 --> 00:35:39,680
and there's no way to really figure it out until you test it.

465
00:35:40,080 --> 00:35:42,880
You talked about it being that trickle over time.

466
00:35:43,600 --> 00:35:56,320
I guess my question was over time for Surfline, as that value prop has increased, as you figured things out, as you've done testing, does that trickle start to become a little bit of a stream and grow into a more meaningful thing, like is that trickle growing?

467
00:35:56,560 --> 00:36:07,920
Like is the freemium model actually working in that way over time where that trickle does become a meaningful part of the business of more and more people subscribing in year one after a habituation.

468
00:36:09,040 --> 00:36:15,040
versus that, like, oh, still 98% of our revenue comes from people who signed up like day one.

