00:37:31,840 --> 00:37:34,560
It was probably Vulcum and you guys should keep advertising on Surfline.

493
00:37:34,640 --> 00:37:35,760
So I'm doing you a favor.

494
00:37:36,320 --> 00:37:36,640
Love it.

495
00:37:37,200 --> 00:37:40,800
Over the past five years, we have seen a pretty big increase in conversion of that Furyak.

496
00:37:41,520 --> 00:37:54,880
Typically, the way it works is like if you launch a new product or we add new cams to a certain area, you'll see a uplift pretty quickly and then it will slow down over time, but your baseline conversion over time continues to improve.

497
00:37:55,200 --> 00:38:12,800
I haven't seen too many instances, not here or even in other products where you launch something and your conversion just kind of doubles overnight or anything like that, but what you'll see is that you'll launch a new product, you'll get a bunch of early adopters that come in, subscribe, but your baseline conversion after those early adopters have kind of normalized is a bit higher than what it was before.

498
00:38:12,960 --> 00:38:20,800
So that's kind of the model is over time you're hoping that every year your freemium conversions are a little bit better and a little bit better and a little bit better, but the size of your free audience has not gone down.

499
00:38:21,120 --> 00:38:24,080
And if you can do that, then you kind of have a recipe for success.

500
00:38:24,240 --> 00:38:24,320
Yeah.

501
00:38:25,040 --> 00:38:28,000
How does ad sales and sponsorships work in that?

502
00:38:28,480 --> 00:38:34,400
Jacob kind of mentioned it, you are monetizing that free base and with unskillable video ads.

503
00:38:34,800 --> 00:38:35,520
The cams are good.

504
00:38:35,680 --> 00:38:36,560
You have to watch the ads.

505
00:38:36,720 --> 00:38:37,280
That's the deal.

506
00:38:37,600 --> 00:38:39,520
How does that side of the monetization play out?

507
00:38:39,840 --> 00:38:42,560
We have an ad sales team and traditionally we.

508
00:38:42,560 --> 00:38:47,680
Traditionally, we went to market and we would sell media packages and that was kind of our strategy and we would monetize the free audience.

509
00:38:47,920 --> 00:38:51,840
But over time, we've slowly started to transition our focus a little bit.

510
00:38:52,160 --> 00:39:03,440
So today, while we still sell traditional media packages, our focus has shifted a little bit to finding ways to connect the consumer and the advertiser for our premium audience in a native way.

511
00:39:04,160 --> 00:39:09,280
And so the best way for us to do that is to find ways for brands to give value directly to the consumer.

512
00:39:09,520 --> 00:39:12,240
So imagine your consumer behind a paywall, brands trying to reach them.

513
00:39:12,320 --> 00:39:13,920
It's not really easy to put ads behind a paywall.

514
00:39:14,480 --> 00:39:15,680
It's not a great user experience.

515
00:39:15,760 --> 00:39:16,560
I don't recommend it.

516
00:39:16,960 --> 00:39:28,000
So instead, what we've been trying to do is find partnership deals with these brands where the brand will give the consumer some kind of exclusive discount or early access or something that the consumer didn't have before, doesn't have access to.

517
00:39:28,080 --> 00:39:32,720
And then we'll market that perk or promotion native to our consumers behind the paywall.

518
00:39:33,200 --> 00:39:35,440
And so again, we're kind of doing both.

519
00:39:35,680 --> 00:39:41,760
We're doing a traditional media strategy, but we're also pivoting in a big way to try to get as much value to the subscribers as possible.

520
00:39:42,240 --> 00:39:43,040
That's our number one goal.

521
00:39:43,200 --> 00:39:44,800
Do you think about those as two separate strategies?

522
00:39:45,440 --> 00:39:55,600
So you're doing more traditional display ads or whatever you guys are using for the non-subscribed audience and then for the subscribed audience also thinking about ways you can tastefully, I'm sure, monetize behind the paywall.

523
00:39:55,920 --> 00:39:57,200
Yeah, it's the same strategy, right?

524
00:39:57,440 --> 00:40:06,640
We're going to brands and trying to figure out if there's an offer that makes sense for them behind the paywall versus in front of the paywall, but usually what we put behind the paywall is very well received, right?

525
00:40:06,880 --> 00:40:14,800
A consumer will come in and they'll like, as an example, maybe they get a discount to a new surf park that opens in their area and it's a surf line only discount.

526
00:40:15,200 --> 00:40:18,480
And so we're not going in there and like bombarding them with ads or anything like that.

527
00:40:18,560 --> 00:40:24,000
We come in and say, hey, this new perk, you guys get early access to the Surf Park or you guys get a discount courtesy of whoever.

528
00:40:24,480 --> 00:40:30,800
And the brand wins because we're monetizing in a native way, which is traditionally, it performs much better than if we just put a banner ad somewhere.

529
00:40:31,120 --> 00:40:35,840
And the consumer wins because they're getting some kind of incremental value that they didn't have before.

530
00:40:36,320 --> 00:40:44,560
I imagine that's a really good audience to sell because you've kind of proven they have some kind of disposable income in terms of CPIs, I guess versus your free audience.

531
00:40:44,720 --> 00:40:45,600
It's going to be pretty good, right?

532
00:40:45,760 --> 00:40:50,480
Well, one I haven't noticed, I've been a premium subscriber, and I was like, I never felt advertised to, so kudos.

533
00:40:50,880 --> 00:40:56,000
But we don't talk about that all that much, David, on this podcast about ways of monetizing a premium audience.

534
00:40:56,160 --> 00:40:58,400
You know, you talk about that ARPU strategy, right?

535
00:40:58,640 --> 00:41:04,160
Increasing your ARPU, at some point your subscription probably reaches the value peak and you won't be able to go much further.

536
00:41:04,640 --> 00:41:11,200
You can start to think creatively about what are other ways that we can engage with our audience, bring them value, again, create value and capture it, right?

537
00:41:11,440 --> 00:41:13,840
Which can be bringing them eating offers and things like this.

538
00:41:14,000 --> 00:41:17,920
And it's probably a little bit of a narrow path to walk, but I think it can be done..

539
00:41:18,400 --> 00:41:27,600
I think the monetizing of the free users and understanding the value, we've talked about that a bit in the past few episodes about understanding the value that those free users are bringing.

540
00:41:27,760 --> 00:41:33,440
And like we were talking about here driving user acquisition without having to do a ton of paid user acquisition, that kind of thing.

541
00:41:33,440 --> 00:41:38,720
It's like when you understand that, it helps inform the strategy for freemium.

542
00:41:39,280 --> 00:41:52,080
But then the other layer that I think you all have figured out that many others haven't is effectively monetizing those free users as well, getting some value back out through these sponsorships, through the unskipable video ads and things like that.

543
00:41:52,320 --> 00:41:53,920
So I think Duolingo is a great example.

544
00:41:54,240 --> 00:41:56,000
They don't monetize that free base at all.

545
00:41:56,400 --> 00:42:02,960
I will be curious, like they just crossed a ten billion dollar valuation, they hit a ceiling on their paid side and start to think about that.

546
00:42:03,200 --> 00:42:17,680
I mean, yeah, we may see some brand partnerships and ads and other things make their way into Duolingo over time as they have to monetize that free user base more strongly to keep the growth going as they've saturated the kind of willingness to pay and whatnot.

547
00:42:17,920 --> 00:42:19,680
But anyway, so much more I'd love to tell you.

